   "text": {
    "L1": "Shapes can be copied on a square or dot grid.",
    "L2": "To copy one exactly, count the units along every side. A unit is a gap between dots, not a dot.",
    "L3": "Here is a rectangle that is 3 squares long and 2 squares tall. It covers 6 small squares.",
    "L4": "On a dot grid, choose a starting dot and go right 3 gaps. Then go up 2 gaps.",
    "L5": "Go left 3 gaps and down 2 gaps to finish back at the start.",
    "L6": "Turning or sliding a shape does not change its side lengths, corners, or the squares it covers.",
    "L7": "Here is the common mistake: five dots in a row do not make 5 units. Count the four gaps between them.",
    "L8": "Now check the line from the first dot to the fifth dot. How many units long is it?"
   },
